# ── Icon font safety net ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Netlify refuses to deploy ANY file inside a node_modules folder, and Expo
# emits vendor assets there. So the Ionicons font that @expo/vector-icons asks
# for by default is never on the server, every icon renders as an empty box,
# and the failed loads retry endlessly — which froze the whole site.
#
# The app itself now loads the font from assets/fonts (see src/app/_layout.tsx),
# so this rule is a backstop: anything that still requests the vendor path gets
# a real font instead of a 404. public/fonts/ionicons.ttf is copied into every
# build, so unlike a patch applied to a single deploy, this survives rebuilds.
#
# Note: this maps the whole vendor Fonts folder to Ionicons, which is correct
# while Ionicons is the only icon set in use. If another set is ever added,
# give it its own rule above this one.
/assets/node_modules/@expo/vector-icons/build/vendor/react-native-vector-icons/Fonts/*   /fonts/ionicons.ttf   200

# ── Assets must never fall through to the app shell ───────────────────────
# Without these, the catch-all below answers a MISSING file with index.html and
# a 200 status. A missing font then arrives as HTML that the browser quietly
# refuses, and icons break with no error anywhere — exactly what happened, and
# it took a manual trace of the response bytes to find. Existing files are
# still served normally; only genuinely missing ones 404.
/assets/*   /not-found.html    404
/_expo/*    /not-found.html    404

# ── Single-page app ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every other route is resolved in the browser by one index.html, so a direct
# visit or hard refresh on /agenda serves the app rather than a 404. Kept here
# (rather than only in netlify.toml) so it is copied into the build and applies
# to drag-and-drop deploys too.
/*          /index.html  200
